<!--
  Themed icon layer, required from Android 13. A single path, as the design spec asks: the system tints
  it flat, so gradients and colour detail would be lost anyway.

  Since 1.3 the silhouette carries the clock knocked out of it, which is the only way to keep the dial
  when everything is one colour. It relies on the nonZero fill rule: subpaths wound the *same* way as the
  body merge into it, and subpaths wound the *opposite* way punch holes. Order: body → calyx-with-stem,
  both hands and the 3/6/9 marks as holes.

  Three traps paid for while drawing it, all invisible until rendered:

  · Calyx and stem are ONE contour, not two. As separate holes they overlap between y=26.4 and 28.4, and
    two holes on top of each other sum to winding −2 — non-zero, so the overlap fills back in and the
    calyx grows a wart. The stem is spliced into the calyx's top centre vertex instead.
  · The hands start at radius 3.75, not at the centre, so they never cross each other — which would hit
    the same −2 problem right in the middle. **The white left between them IS the pin**: there is no
    separate pin subpath here, because it would have to be an island inside two overlapping holes.
  · Winding direction is computed, not guessed: each contour's signed area is compared against the
    body's. Reversing the *normal* of a capsule does not reverse its winding — it mirrors it, which is
    how the hands first came out filled instead of hollow.

  There is no mark at twelve o'clock: it would sit under the stem, so in the colour icon it is invisible
  ink and here it would collide with the same hole. The stem is the twelve.
-->
